window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.4","title":"The Normal Distribution","goal":"Use the heights of adult men (mean 69.1 in, SD 2.9 in) to find the share shorter than 66 inches and the height of the 90th percentile.","question":"The heights of adult men are roughly bell shaped with a mean of 69.1 inches and a standard deviation of 2.9 inches. A clothing company asks: what share of men are shorter than 66 inches, and what height marks the tallest 10%? Can the normal distribution answer this, and does the 68-95-99.7 rule hold?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Describe the bell curve","text":"A normal distribution is fixed by two numbers: its centre (the mean) and its width (the standard deviation). SciPy bundles both into one object we can ask questions of.","code":"from scipy import stats\n\nmean, sd = 69.1, 2.9\nheights = stats.norm(loc=mean, scale=sd)\n\nprint('median =', heights.median())","explain":["Load the statistics tools from SciPy.","Store the mean and standard deviation: the centre and the width of the bell curve.","Build a normal distribution with that centre and width.","Print the median. For a normal curve it equals the mean."],"meaning":"The median is 69.1, the same as the mean. A normal curve is symmetric, so half of men are shorter than 69.1 inches and half are taller.","out":"median = 69.1","figs":[]},{"title":"Share shorter than 66 inches","text":"cdf gives the area under the curve to the left of a value, which is the probability of being at or below it. The z-score says how many standard deviations 66 is below the mean.","code":"z = (66 - mean) / sd\nshare = heights.cdf(66)\n\nprint(f'z = {z:.2f}')\nprint(f'P(height < 66) = {share:.4f}')","explain":["Convert 66 into a z-score: how many standard deviations it lies from the mean.","Ask for the area to the left of 66 with cdf (cumulative distribution function).","Print the z-score.","Print the probability of being shorter than 66 inches."],"meaning":"The z-score is -1.07, so 66 inches is a bit more than 1 SD below the mean. The area to its left is 0.1425, so about 14.3% of men are shorter than 66 inches.","out":"z = -1.07\nP(height < 66) = 0.1425","figs":[]},{"title":"The 90th percentile","text":"ppf runs the other way: give it an area and it returns the value. Only 10% of men are taller than the height it prints.","code":"p90 = heights.ppf(0.90)\n\nprint(f'90th percentile = {p90:.1f} inches')","explain":["ppf (the percent point function) runs cdf backwards: give an area, get a value.","Print the 90th percentile in inches."],"meaning":"The 90th percentile is 72.8 inches. Only 10% of men are taller than that, and 90% are shorter.","out":"90th percentile = 72.8 inches","figs":[]},{"title":"Check the 68-95-99.7 rule","text":"The empirical rule says about 68%, 95% and 99.7% of values lie within 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations of the mean. We check it with the standard normal curve.","code":"for k in (1, 2, 3):\n    inside = stats.norm.cdf(k) - stats.norm.cdf(-k)\n    print(f'within {k} SD: {inside:.4f}')","explain":["Loop over 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations.","The area between -k and +k is the area to the left of +k minus the area to the left of -k.","Print each result."],"meaning":"About 0.6827 of values lie within 1 SD, 0.9545 within 2 SD and 0.9973 within 3 SD. These are the exact versions of the 68-95-99.7 rule.","out":"within 1 SD: 0.6827\nwithin 2 SD: 0.9545\nwithin 3 SD: 0.9973","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw it","text":"The shaded area is the probability we computed for men shorter than 66 inches. A picture makes it clear that probabilities are areas.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nx = np.linspace(mean - 4 * sd, mean + 4 * sd, 300)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(x, heights.pdf(x))\nplt.fill_between(x, heights.pdf(x), where=x < 66, alpha=0.4)\nplt.xlabel('height (inches)')\nplt.show()","explain":["Load NumPy for the grid of x-values.","Load matplotlib for plotting.","Make 300 evenly spaced heights across four standard deviations either side of the mean.","Start a figure with a size that fits on the page.","Draw the bell curve: the height of the curve is the probability density.","Shade the area to the left of 66. That area is the probability 0.1425.","Label the x-axis with the units.","Display the figure."],"meaning":"The curve is highest at 69.1 and the shaded area on the left is the 14.25% of men shorter than 66 inches. A probability is an area under the curve.","out":"","figs":["3.4-5.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"pnorm(66, mean = 69.1, sd = 2.9)\nqnorm(0.90, mean = 69.1, sd = 2.9)\npnorm(1) - pnorm(-1)\npnorm(2) - pnorm(-2)","explain":["pnorm gives the area to the left of 66, the share shorter than 66 inches.","qnorm runs backwards: the height with 90% of men below it.","Area within 1 SD of the mean on the standard normal.","Area within 2 SD of the mean."],"expect":"0.1425; 72.82; 0.6827; 0.9545","read":"The four numbers are 0.1425 (share below 66 inches), 72.82 (90th percentile), 0.6827 and 0.9545. They match Python.","note":"pnorm gives the area to the left of a value, and qnorm is its inverse."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use the CDF.NORMAL and IDF.NORMAL functions)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE p_below_66 = CDF.NORMAL(66, 69.1, 2.9).\nCOMPUTE height_p90 = IDF.NORMAL(0.90, 69.1, 2.9).\nCOMPUTE within_1sd = CDF.NORMAL(1, 0, 1) - CDF.NORMAL(-1, 0, 1).\nFORMATS p_below_66 height_p90 within_1sd (F8.4).\nLIST p_below_66 height_p90 within_1sd.","explain":["Make a tiny data set so there is one row to compute in.","Start the data block.","One row containing the number 1.","End the data block.","CDF.NORMAL(value, mean, sd) is the area to the left of 66.","IDF.NORMAL(area, mean, sd) is the inverse: the 90th percentile height.","Area within 1 SD of the mean, as a difference of two CDF values.","Show four decimals.","Print the results."],"read":"LIST shows p_below_66 = 0.1425, height_p90 = 72.8165 and within_1sd = 0.6827. These match Python's results.","note":"CDF.NORMAL(value, mean, sd) is the area to the left; IDF.NORMAL(area, mean, sd) is the inverse."},"stata":{"code":"display normal((66 - 69.1) / 2.9)\ndisplay 69.1 + 2.9 * invnormal(0.90)\ndisplay normal(1) - normal(-1)","explain":["normal(z) gives the area to the left of a z-score, here (66 - 69.1) / 2.9.","invnormal(0.90) is the z-score for the 90th percentile; we convert back with mean + sd x z.","Area within 1 SD of the mean."],"read":"The three lines print .14254 (share below 66), 72.81650 (90th percentile) and .68268949 (within 1 SD). They match Python after rounding.","note":"normal(z) is the area to the left of a z-score and invnormal(p) is its inverse, so convert to and from z by hand."},"conclusion":"About 14.3% of men are shorter than 66 inches, and the 90th percentile of height is 72.8 inches. The rule of thumb holds: 68.3%, 95.4% and 99.7% of men are within 1, 2 and 3 SD of the mean. The caution: this assumes heights follow a normal curve, which is only an approximation for real people.","practice":{"scenario":"A delivery company finds that trips to a suburb take on average 32 minutes with an SD of 6 minutes, and the times are roughly bell shaped. The dispatcher wants to promise a time that almost all trips meet and to know how often a trip is very quick or in a normal range.","data":"Mean = 32 minutes, SD = 6 minutes, normal distribution. Cut-offs: 25 minutes, 28 to 40 minutes, the 95th percentile and the middle 95%.","tasks":["Find the z-score of 25 minutes and the share of trips shorter than 25 minutes.","Find the 95th percentile of trip times.","Find the share of trips between 28 and 40 minutes.","Find the minutes that bracket the middle 95% of trips."],"hint":"Build stats.norm(loc=32, scale=6). Use cdf for areas, a difference of two cdf values for between, and ppf (with 0.025 and 0.975 for the middle 95%) for percentiles.","solution":{"code":"from scipy import stats\n\ntrip = stats.norm(loc=32, scale=6)\nprint(f'z for 25 min      = {(25 - 32) / 6:.2f}')\nprint(f'P(time < 25)      = {trip.cdf(25):.4f}')\nprint(f'95th percentile   = {trip.ppf(0.95):.1f} minutes')\nprint(f'P(28 < time < 40) = {trip.cdf(40) - trip.cdf(28):.4f}')\nprint(f'middle 95% runs from {trip.ppf(0.025):.1f} to {trip.ppf(0.975):.1f} minutes')","answer":"The z-score is -1.17 and P(time < 25) = 0.1217, so about 12% of trips are that quick. The 95th percentile is 41.9 minutes, so a promise of 42 minutes is met by 95% of trips. P(28 < time < 40) = 0.6563. The middle 95% runs from 20.2 to 43.8 minutes, roughly the mean plus or minus 2 SD.","out":"z for 25 min      = -1.17\nP(time < 25)      = 0.1217\n95th percentile   = 41.9 minutes\nP(28 < time < 40) = 0.6563\nmiddle 95% runs from 20.2 to 43.8 minutes","figs":[]}}});
